cartridge "JERICHO II/ROBERT BIXBY/1989"

' JERICHO II
' A ColecoVision game based on "Jericho II" by Robert Bixby, COMPUTE!'s
' Gazette June 1989 (Commodore 64 machine language, SYS 2288; the author's
' name is in the program's own text, dated Christmas 1988). Made for Amy
' Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a byte-for-byte port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' machine code, orig/jericho2.dis, and watched in jsc64), the game is
' rebuilt for the ColecoVision: balls as smooth sprites, cut-stone bricks
' that crack and change colour as they weaken, grooved tracks, sound.
'
' The game: a duel for two (or one against the computer). The screen is
' split in two halves; each holds a player's wall and is ringed by that
' player's track, which carries two shields on opposite sides. Turn your
' ring with the stick to keep the balls in your half: every ball that
' touches a brick of your wall knocks it and shakes a few more at random.
' A ball that slips through a gap in your ring (top, bottom or the middle)
' flies into your opponent's half and works for them. Bring your wall down
' five times (each new wall is harder) before the other player does.
'
' From the original (orig/): two halves, each a ring of track round a wall
' (C64: 20 x 24 cells, ring of 84 with two 10-cell shields opposite each
' other, wall 10 x 12 in the middle; here 14 x 21, ring 66, shields 8,
' wall 8 x 9 with the same free border proportions); the stick turns the
' whole ring, both shields together; the balls move diagonally a cell at a
' time (C64: a cell every 4 sixtieths, every 3 from level 3; here 6 and
' 5, see the liberties), bounce off
' shields, bricks, other balls and the screen's outer edges, and pass
' through the gaps: down past the bottom row a ball comes in two rows from
' the top of the other half, up past the top two rows from the bottom of
' the other half, sideways through the middle; bricks take 3 + L hits at
' level L (C64: colour 6 + hits up to 9), and every brick hit also knocks
' 5 - L random bricks of the same wall; balls in play: (L1 + L2) / 2 + 1,
' at most 4, each new one starting from its own corner; every 4 ball steps
' each fallen brick of a wall scores 10 ^ L for its player (ten-digit
' scores, the C64 had eleven), then a wall with no brick left is rebuilt
' one level harder; the left player is checked first; five walls win.
'
' Liberties: the balls are slower, a cell every 6 frames (5 once either
' player reaches level 3; C64: 4 and 3): at the C64's speed a player who
' sees the balls a quarter second late did no better than one who never
' touched the stick (sim.mjs, on the exact rules of model.mjs: 4 wins in
' 10 against an idle ring; at 6/5, 9 in 10); the ring turns 2 cells every
' 3 frames while the stick is held (C64: about 48 cells a second on a ring
' of 84, as fast as its main loop ran); balls glide between cells; one
' sound a frame (the loudest news: a fallen brick over a knock); the right edge bounce is a mirror of
' the left one (C64: a ball at the right edge jumped back three cells);
' the random knocks pick among all standing bricks (C64: rows 1-11 of 12,
' and it hung when only the bottom row was left); a ball meeting another
' ball only bounces (C64: it also counted as a brick hit); the faster
' ball speed starts when either player reaches level 3 (C64: whichever
' player scored last); a computer can play the right half (it rethinks
' every 6 frames and ignores bricks in its guess); the game ends
' with a winner's message (C64: PLAY AGAIN? Y/N); the attract mode's
' self-turning rings are left out; sounds and tune are new.
'
' Controls: joystick only. Up or right turns your ring clockwise, down or
' left counter-clockwise. Left half: controller 1, right half: controller
' 2 (one player: either controller plays the left half). On the title,
' up and down choose, a button starts.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` after every `clear
' sprites`. Own xorshift random numbers.

const TBrick1 = 128
const TBrick2 = 129
const TBrick3 = 130
const TBrick4 = 131
const TBrick5 = 132
const TBrick6 = 133
const TBrick7 = 134
const TTrackh = 135
const TTrackv = 136
const TTrackc = 137
const TShhl = 138
const TShvl = 139
const TShcl = 140
const TShhr = 141
const TShvr = 142
const TShcr = 143
const TLogo = 144
const TLogo2 = 145
const THorn = 146
const SBall = 0
const NTiles8 = 152
const SprBytes = 32
const TitleRows = 14
const SndTuneA = 1
const SndTuneB = 2
const SndPing = 3
const SndKnock = 4
const SndFall = 5
const SndHorns = 6
const SndWin = 7
const SndPick = 8
const NRing = 66
const SegL = 8
const HalfR = 33
const NBr = 72
const WallW = 8
const WallC = 3
const WallR = 6
const LastR = 20
const LastC = 27
const MsgRow = 11
const PerA = 6
const PerB = 5

data BallCol bytes 15,11,7,9
' each ball's corner: row, column, sideways (1 right), up/down (1 down)
data BallR0 bytes 10,10,3,3
data BallC0 bytes 2,25,25,2
data BallDx0 bytes 0,1,1,0
data BallDy0 bytes 0,0,1,1
data Glide5 bytes 0,2,3,5,6,8
data Glide6 bytes 0,1,3,4,5,7,8
data ShTile bytes TShhl,TShvl,TShcl,TShhr,TShvr,TShcr

